# LaTeX build artifacts and scratch renders (regenerate with: python build.py)
build/
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz

# No assembled volume and no PDF is ever published. Only the Markdown chapters
# and the projects are, which is what mdbuild.py writes. These are blanket rules
# rather than named files, so a new output name cannot slip through: the whole
# volume, a single chapter, or anything else.
*.pdf
*.html

# Named explicitly as well, so the intent survives anyone relaxing the rules above.
firmware-nucleo-h7.pdf
firmware-nucleo-h7.html
chapter-*.pdf
chapter-*.html

# Local virtual environments and editor state
.venv/
venv/
.vscode/
.idea/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The Rust workspace's build output. One directory for every crate, at the
# repository root, which is where cargo puts it for a workspace. The Cargo.lock
# beside it IS committed: this workspace produces a binary whose figures are
# quoted, so the dependency set is pinned rather than resolved afresh.
/target/

# Host build output for the code half. Kept out of build/, which is the LaTeX
# scratch directory, so neither can delete the other's work.
build-host/
*.o
*.obj
*.so
*.dll
*.exe
*.elf
*.map
*.stale-*
.pytest_cache/

# Captures. A capture without its build identity is not evidence, and one with
# it is too large and too specific to belong in the history.
*.csv
*.npz
captures/

# Journals and correspondence never reach a published repository, private
# shares included. They live in the catalogue, which is local only. A published
# repository carries the work, never the running record of doing it. This is a
# guard rather than a reminder: the rule has held by discipline so far, and a
# rule that lives only in someone's head is one copy away from being broken.
private_journal/
correspondence/
DIARY*.md

# The authoring sources stay out of the published tree. A reader of this
# repository wants a chapter, which is a Markdown file with its figures as SVG
# beside it; the LaTeX that generates one shows raw markup and helps nobody.
# The assembled PDF and the single-file HTML are local for the same reason.
#
# This is a blanket rule rather than named folders, so a new source file
# cannot slip through: sections/, figures/*.tex, main.tex and the preamble are
# all covered by the one line.
#
# The cost is named rather than hidden. With the sources ignored, editing one
# never shows in git status, so nothing reminds anybody to rebuild. That is
# what prepublish.py is for, and it regenerates before it checks anything.
*.tex
